course: dictionary
lesson: 14002
title: Lubricate and the clues around it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from machines, bodies and everyday life, and the clues around them can reveal their meaning. «Dad put oil on the squeaky gate so it would swing smoothly.» Squeaky tells you something was rubbing, and the oil made it move easily. So lubricate means to put oil or grease on something so it moves smoothly.
explain: «The bike chain was dry, so Sam had to lubricate it before pedalling up the hill.» Dry and hard pedalling are the clues. A dry chain rubs and is hard to move, so to lubricate it is to make it slippery so it moves easily. Test it: "Sam had to make it slippery before pedalling" still makes sense.
explain: Lubricate can also describe your body. «Tears lubricate your eyes, so they do not feel dry when you blink.» Dry eyes feel uncomfortable, so the tears keep them moist and slippery. Lubricate can even be informal: «The hot chocolate helped lubricate the chat between the two new neighbours» means it made the chat go more smoothly.
fact: Lubricate means to put oil, grease or another slippery stuff on something so it moves smoothly.
fact: «Lubricate» is a verb, so it changes: lubricates, lubricated, lubricating.
fact: The noun «lubricant» is the oil or grease itself, and «lubrication» is the action of lubricating.
fact: In science, lubricating reduces friction, the rubbing force that slows moving things down.
fact: In your body, fluid lubricates your joints, and tears lubricate your eyes.
exercise: «Mia used oil to lubricate the bike chain.» What does lubricate mean here? | To wash something in cold water | *To make something slippery so it moves smoothly | To paint something bright red | To break something into small pieces
exercise: «Tears lubricate your eyes.» What job do tears do? | They stop light getting in | They make the eye close tightly | *They keep the eye moist and slippery | They give the eye its colour
exercise: Which word is the oil or grease itself? | lubricated | lubricating | *lubricant | lubricates
test: «The team tried to lubricate the talk with a few jokes.» What does lubricate mean here? | *To make the talk go more smoothly | To end the talk quickly | To write the talk down | To cover the talk in oil
test: Which word names the action of lubricating? | lubricate | lubricant | *lubrication | lubricated
exam: Why do engineers lubricate the moving parts of a machine? | To make the parts louder | *To reduce friction so the parts move smoothly and wear less | To stop the machine from ever moving | To make the parts heavier
